WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.509
The harsh truth of the film industry is that
movies are business investments.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:08.440
In the best-case scenario, a movie thrills
audiences and makes producers happier and

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:08.940
wealthier.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.880
But sometimes a movie can be so bad, so bizarre,
and so damaging to the reputation of a studio

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.180
that the people in charge will willingly kill
it, keeping it away from audiences and writing

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.480
it off as a lost cause.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.500
Here are a few movies that, if the studios
had their way, the public would never, ever

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.140
get to see.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.880
"Here is entertainment so entertaining, so
uplifting, so satifactual, you'll want to

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.140
see it again and again."

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:34.420
Rad

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.080
Released in 1986, Rad follows a young, unsigned
BMXer striving to come out on top in the world's

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.660
greatest off-road bike race.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.120
It's gnarly with a capital G, and its camp
value is off the charts.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.400
"Would you try to understand the only thing
I'm good at is racing this bike!"

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.720
Reviewers hated it, blaming stilted performances,
and a totally inexplicable plot.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.231
Viewers with a healthy sense of irony, however,
can still find plenty to appreciate, like

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.880
a scene in which characters take their bikes
to the school dance — and onto the dance

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.380
floor.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.180
Rad may fail at what it's trying to do, but
it goes above and beyond as a relic of its

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.560
era, so it's well worth tracking down online
or on unofficial DVDs.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.740
It was popular as a rental in the days of
VHS, but the studio has all but abandoned

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.030
it since — unfortunate, considering its
growing value as an examination of '80s culture

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.580
at its most totally radical.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.140
Martin

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.800
One of the late George Romero's best and most
interesting horror movies is also his hardest

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.540
to track down.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.880
You can find a copy of Night of the Living
Dead anywhere, but if you want to legitimately

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.380
see Martin, you'll have to pony up for an
out-of-print DVD or track down a bootleg copy.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.870
The story of a young man in Pittsburgh who
may or may not be an ageless vampire, Martin

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.080
is a movie that sticks with you.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.700
Its hero, who appears unsure as to whether
or not he's a monster, is sweet and sympathetic,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.140
which makes the scenes of him drugging, cutting,
and drinking the blood from people all the

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:51.940
more disquieting.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.640
"Don't be afraid.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.640
You'll just go to sleep.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.500
I don't have to hurt you now."

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.540
It's not the quality of the film that keeps
it out of viewers' hands, because it's actually

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.480
pretty highly regarded among its fans.

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:10.200
It's reportedly not available because the
producer who owns the rights for its release

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.760
simply wants too much money for it.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.300
It's a shame it's out of print, because it's
a unique, wonderfully crafted take on the

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.100
vampire fable, and an example of how to remix
old monster stories in a hauntingly effective

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:20.600
way.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.000
The Last Movie

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.620
There's a lot of interesting things going
on in Dennis Hopper's second feature, 1971's

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.320
The Last Movie.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.120
The problem is, they're not presented in an
interesting way.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.760
You really can't fault Universal Pictures
for burying this movie and never releasing

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:34.580
it on DVD.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.900
Roger Ebert wasn't kidding when he summed
it up as "a wasteland of cinematic wreckage."

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.901
It's even edited out of order to the point
of incoherence; the title card for the movie

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:44.660
doesn't appear until 26 minutes in.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.200
"And I want it legitimate and different.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.960
And better than its ever been done."

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.320
A few viewings could help you cobble together
the meaning of the movie, which follows Hopper's

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.680
character as he goes native in Peru and contends
with locals who can't tell that movies aren't

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.180
reality.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.380
But it's a joyless, aggravating experience.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.180
Despite the criticism, and the fact that the
film's performance kept Hopper out of the

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.739
director's chair for nearly a decade afterward,
Hopper supported the movie up to his final

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.900
days, screening it on his own while trying
to secure a home video revival.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:13.320
Let It Be

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.620
The film that captured the Beatles struggling
to recapture their chemistry, Let It Be has

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.800
developed a reputation over the years as a
portrait of a band dissolving.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.280
In reality, it's not as explosive as has been
implied, unless you were naive enough to think

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.480
the bandmates never bickered.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.480
While it may have been a bummer at the time,
the movie is an interesting, loosely-constructed

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.840
chronicle of the recording sessions leading
up to their legendary final rooftop performance.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.710
It doesn't make anybody look good, but it
doesn't make anyone look that bad, either,

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:42.420
and any Beatles fan is apt to find something
intriguing in the film's many moments.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.620
Whether it's the implied drama of Yoko Ono
lurking at the fringes of the studio or the

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:50.730
way the band lights up when it comes time
to play some music, it's an intriguing document,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.720
with the rooftop performance serving as an
unlikely swan song to one of the most successful

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.080
musical runs of all time.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.960
So why hasn't it ever released on DVD or Blu-ray?

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.200
While it's consistently been suppressed through
threat of lawsuit by its image-conscious cast,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:07.180
the reality today is that — for a layman
audience — the movie's just not very good

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.020
as a movie.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.400
It's got an aimless structure and lousy sound.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.120
It's a fans-only experience of a depressing
time that a lot of casual music-lovers probably

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.060
wouldn't care to see.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.900
The Star Wars trilogy

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.560
In what is perhaps the biggest, strangest
case of cinematic injustice on this list,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.710
the original three Star Wars movies — undoubtedly
some of the most influential films of all

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:30.060
time — are extremely difficult to find in
their original form.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:34.420
For decades now, each release of the movies
has shown incremental changes in their content,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.380
all in the name of moving, quote, "closer"
to what's been called George Lucas' original

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:38.880
vision.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.280
The most egregious examples of this cinematic
revisionism were the Star Wars special editions,

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.120
released in 1997 in an overt attempt to replace,
rather than complement, the trilogy.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.580
As those special editions were developed,
it became increasingly harder for actual,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.120
original versions of the movies to be found.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.160
The shortage of official releases has led
to fan edits of the special editions, which

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.540
keep the cleaned-up visuals and sound while
ditching the extraneous junk.

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:05.759
The fact that fans have had to do this with
some of the highest-grossing, most culturally-embedded

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:10.080
movies ever is nothing short of absurd, and
the day Disney releases the originals in a

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.360
restored format will be a cause for celebration.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.220
Speaking of Star Wars celebrations...

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.500
The Star Wars Holiday Special

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.040
With a legendary reputation in the world of
bad entertainment, the Star Wars Holiday Special

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.680
is so far afield from what makes the franchise
great that it boggles the mind.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.480
Released after the success of the first movie,
this largely plotless, overlong, ugly, and

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:34.840
aggressively unentertaining slog poses itself
as a variety show in space, which leads to

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.210
surreal moments like Bea Arthur singing to
aliens in a cantina, and Wookies watching

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.980
what can only be described as holographic
dance porno.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:46.480
"I am your experience, so experience me."

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.420
"I am your pleasure.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.180
So enjoy me."

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.180
It's frequently been reported that George
Lucas has said that if he had the time, he

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.969
would destroy every copy in existence — and,
really, someone should've let him.

00:05:58.969 --> 00:06:01.220
Ironically or not, there's no fun to be had
here.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.979
The Star Wars Holiday Special's biggest problem
is that it commits the cardinal sin of the

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.800
crappy movie: it's not just bad.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.020
It's boring, too.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.660
"Where's Chewbacca?"

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.200
The Ewoks films

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:17.100
Less notorious than the despised Holiday Special,
the Ewok films are interesting Star Wars apocrypha.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:21.039
They happen on a pretty faithful version of
Endor, and actor Warwick Davis returns as

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:23.469
Wicket, who made his first appearance in Return
of the Jedi.

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:23.969
The problem?

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.119
Well, the Ewoks are more articulate than they
were in Jedi, and hanging around with a stranded

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:28.860
human family.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.160
"We came on a star cruiser and we crashed!"

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:34.720
"(Speaking Ewokese)"

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:41.640
Even less tolerable is the decidedly non-Star
Wars plot turns both movies take.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:46.639
The first film, Caravan of Courage, includes
a magical pond that traps people, bootleg-looking

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:50.710
fairies made of light, and the unbearable
moral message that love is the strongest force

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:51.500
in the universe.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.800
What is this, Interstellar?

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:54.840
"Love, TARS, love.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:57.900
My connection with Murph.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:58.620
It is quantifiable."

00:06:58.960 --> 00:06:59.460
Shut up!

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.060
Anyway, Caravan of Courage's sequel, The Battle
for Endor, immediately goes full oddball with

00:07:04.069 --> 00:07:07.949
the introduction of an evil, shapeshifting
sorceress whose outfit looks like something

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:12.020
the costuming department on Xena: Warrior
Princess would've rejected as too tacky.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:16.539
There's also a horrifying Alf-like alien that
lead actor Wilford Brimley's character lives

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:17.540
with in the woods.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:21.899
Easy to make fun of, they're entertaining
experiences if you watch with the proper mindset.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.160
But are they're not actually good.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:24.200
Or interesting.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.620
Hey, even the prequels can't live up to that
standard.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:27.700
The Carter

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:32.000
This documentary is a fairly straightforward
look at the life of the rapper Lil Wayne,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.540
recorded around the the release of Tha Carter
III in 2008.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.820
What makes The Carter such an interesting
document is how revealing it is.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:42.100
It's remarkably unflattering to its subject,
and the damage is all self-inflicted.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:47.320
Even as it demonstrates how talented the rapper
is at what he does, Wayne's selfishness, self-destructive

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:50.480
habits, and personal rudeness make him a very
difficult figure to like.

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:54.240
A look into the mind and life of an artist
you would probably never want to meet, it's

00:07:54.241 --> 00:07:58.639
a great watch, and though it's been sporadically
released on iTunes, it's currently not officially

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.980
available, with legal challenges from Wayne's
camp blocking the release of the film.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:03.600
Song of the South

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.740
Though the notion that Disney's Song of the
South takes place during the times of American

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.880
slavery is a misconception, the reality isn't
much better.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:15.259
This is radioactive material, and Disney's
portrayal of the post-Civil War South as a

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:19.520
chipper place where former slaves sit around
singing songs all night feels like its glossing

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:20.540
over the big picture.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:24.459
The story of Song of the South is actually
about a young white boy and his relationship

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:26.200
with Uncle Remus, a former slave.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:30.120
In the film, the white people are without
a doubt the bad guys and Uncle Remus is the

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:30.620
hero.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.560
But none of that matters, because the setting
is so jarring that it's not shocking at all

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.780
that Disney doesn't want it to re-emerge as
something people can buy.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.860
As of the making of this video, Song of the
South never been released officially on home

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.280
video in the United States.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:47.960
In all likelihood, unless Disney experiences
a drastic change in its artistic mission,

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:49.420
it probably never will be..

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:50.800
Glitterati

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:56.200
Roger Avary's adaptation of Bret Easton Ellis'
The Rules of Attraction is an underappreciated

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.800
dark comedy, bearing a loose and loopy structure
filmed with daring stylistic touches.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:04.380
One of the more memorable sequences occurs
in the middle of the film, when a world-travelling

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:08.510
character named Victor recounts his extensive
European travels in a breathless, kinetic

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:09.010
montage.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.220
Though the sequence barely lasts four minutes,
Avary reportedly shot days' worth of footage

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:17.560
for it, with Victor's actor staying in character
as he takes drugs, seduces women, and otherwise

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.090
behaves like the alpha and omega of the boorish
American tourist archetype.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.380
Avary assembled this footage into its own
movie, called Glitterati, which is one of

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.590
the more legendarily hard-to-see films from
recent years, which author Ellis describes

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:32.440
as "like a documentary with a fictional character
in the middle of it."

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.160
Lionsgate Films distributed The Rules of Attraction,
but not Glitterati, and the reasons come down

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:39.980
to what is likely a combination of quality
and legality.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:40.980
According to Ellis,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.890
"...for many legal reasons, it will never
see the light of day.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.410
You can't really show Glitterati in public,
it's not possible.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:48.780
There are a lot of people who would be very
upset."

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.560
Avary himself has called the process of making
Glitterati "insane" and "ethically questionable"

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:57.310
— to this date, he has only showed the film
at screenings that he hosts, and the chances

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:00.140
of that changing anytime soon are vanishingly
small.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:01.000
Thanks for watching!

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.430
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:05.570
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

